1 NAME 惟仁 /清和天皇/
2 GIVN 惟仁
2 _MARNM 清和天皇
1 NAME Korehito Seiwa-tenno
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 10 MAY 850
1 DEAT
2 DATE 7 JAN 881
1 OCCU 56th Emperor of Japan
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Seiwa
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Seiwa (清和天皇, Seiwa-tennō, May 10, 850 – January 7, 881) was the 56th emperor of Japan,[1] according to the traditional order of succession.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Seiwa's reign spanned the years from 858 through 876.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Traditional narrative
2 CONT Seiwa was the fourth son of Emperor Montoku. His mother was Empress Dowager Fujiwara no Akirakeiko (明子), also called the Somedono empress (染殿后). Seiwa's mother was the daughter of Fujiwara no Yoshifusa (藤原良房), who was regent and g
2 CONC reat minister of the council of state.[4] He was the younger half-brother of Imperial Prince Koretaka (惟喬親王; 844–897)
2 CONT
2 CONT Imina
2 CONT Before his ascension to the Chrysanthemum Throne, his personal name (his imina)[5] was Korehito (惟仁),[6] the first member of the Imperial house to be personally named "-hito" 仁. One meaning of the character 仁 is the Confucian conc
2 CONC ept of ren. Later it has been a tradition to name the personal name of all male members of the Imperial family this way.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also known as emperor as Mizunoo-no-mikado[4] or Minoo-tei.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Events of Seiwa's life
2 CONT Originally under the guardianship of his maternal grandfather Fujiwara no Yoshifusa, he displaced Imperial Prince Koretaka (惟喬親王) as Crown Prince. Upon the death of his father in 858, Emperor Montoku, he became Emperor at the age 
2 CONC of 9, but the real power was held by his grandfather, Yoshifusa.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 October 858 (Ten'an 2, 27th day of the 8th month): In the 8th year of Montoku-tennō's reign (文徳天皇8年), the emperor died;[6] and the succession (senso) was received by his son. Shortly thereafter, Emperor Seiwa is said to have acc
2 CONC eded to the throne (sokui).[8]
2 CONT 15 December 858 (Ten'an 2, 7th day of the 11th month): The emperor's official announcement of his enthronement at age 9 was accompanied by the appointment of his grandfather as regent (sesshō). This is the first time that this hig
2 CONC h honor has been accorded to a member of the Fujiwara family, and it is also the first example in Japan of the accession of an heir who is too young to be emperor. The proclamation of the beginning of Seiwa's reign was made at the
2 CONC  Kotaijingu at Ise Province and at all the tombs of the imperial family.[9]
2 CONT 859 (Jōgan 1, 1st month): All New Year's festivities were suspended because of the period of national mourning for the death of Emperor Montoku.[10]
2 CONT 859 (Jōgan 1): Construction began on the Iwashimizu Shrine near Heian-kyō. This shrine honors Hachiman, the Shinto war god.[11]
2 CONT 869 (Jōgan 10): Yōzei was born, and he was named Seiwa's heir in the following year.[12]
2 CONT 876 (Jōgan 17, 11th month): In the 18th year of Seiwa-tennō's reign (清和天皇18年), the emperor ceded his throne to his five-year-old son, which meant that the young child received the succession (senso). Shortly thereafter, Emperor Yō
2 CONC zei formally acceded to the throne (sokui).[13]
2 CONT 878 (Gangyō 2): Seiwa became a Buddhist priest. His new priestly name was Soshin (素真).[11]
2 CONT 7 January 881 (Gangyō 4, 4th day of the 12th month): Former-Emperor Seiwa died at age 30.[14]
2 CONT Mausoleum
2 CONT The actual site of Seiwa's grave is known.[1] The emperor is traditionally venerated at the misasagi memorial shrine in the Ukyō-ku ward of Kyoto. The Imperial Household Agency designates this location as Seiwa's mausoleum. It is 
2 CONC formally named the Minooyama no Misasagi (清和天皇陵) or Seiwa Tennō Ryō.[15][16] From the site of his tomb the Emperor Seiwa is sometimes referred to as the Emperor Mizunoo (水尾帝, Mizunoo-tei).[17] The kami of Emperor Seiwa is venerate
2 CONC d at the Seiwatennō-sha near the mausoleum.[18][19]
2 CONT
2 CONT Kugyō
2 CONT Kugyō (公卿) is a collective term for the very few most powerful men attached to the court of the Emperor of Japan in pre-Meiji eras.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT In general, this elite group included only three to four men at a time. These were hereditary courtiers whose experience and background would have brought them to the pinnacle of a life's career. During Seiwa's reign, this apex of
2 CONC  the Daijō-kan included:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sesshō, Fujiwara no Yoshifusa, 804–872.[11]
2 CONT Daijō-daijin, Fujiwara no Yoshifusa.[11]
2 CONT Sadaijin, Minamoto no Makoto (源信).
2 CONT Sadaijin, Minamoto no Tooru (源融).[21]
2 CONT Udaijin, Fujiwara no Yoshimi (藤原良相), 817–867.[22]
2 CONT Udaijin, Fujiwara no Ujimune (藤原氏宗).
2 CONT Udaijin, Fujiwara no Mototsune, 836–891.[23]
2 CONT Naidaijin
2 CONT Dainagon, Fujiwara no Mototsune.[21]
2 CONT Eras of Seiwa's reign
2 CONT The years of Seiwa's reign are more specifically identified by more than one era name or nengō.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ten'an (857–859)
2 CONT Jōgan (859–877)
2 CONT Consorts and children
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo) later Kōtaigō: Fujiwara no Takako (藤原高子; 842–910) later Nijo-kisaki (二条后), Fujiwara no Nagara's daughter
2 CONT First Son: Imperial Prince Sadaakira (貞明親王) later Emperor Yōzei
2 CONT Fourth Son: Imperial Prince Sadayasu (貞保親王; 870–924)
2 CONT Third/Fifth daughter: Imperial Princess Atsuko (敦子内親王; d. 930), 7th Saiin in Kamo Shrine 877–880
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Fujiwara no Tamiko (藤原多美子; d. 886), Fujiwara no Yoshimi's daughter
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Taira no Kanshi (平寛子)
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Princess Kashi (嘉子女王)
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Minamoto no Sadako (源貞子; d. 873)
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Princess Ryūshi (隆子女王)
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Princess Kenshi (兼子女王)
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Princess Chūshi/Tadako (忠子女王; 854–904), Emperor Kōkō's daughter
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Fujiwara no Yoriko (藤原頼子; d. 936), Fujiwara no Mototsune's daughter
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Fujiwara no Kazuko (藤原佳珠子; b. 856), Fujiwara no Mototsune's daughter
2 CONT Seventh Son: Imperial Prince Sadatoki (貞辰親王; 874–929)
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Minamoto no Takeko/Izuko (源厳子; d. 879), Minamoto no Yoshiari's daughter
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Minamoto no Seishi (源済子), Emperor Montoku's daughter
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Minamoto no Kenshi/Atsuko (源喧子)
2 CONT Consort (Nyōgo): Minamoto no Gishi/Yoshiko (源宜子), Minamoto no Okimoto's daughter
2 CONT Court Attendant (Koui): Ariwara no Fumiko (在原文子), Ariwara no Yukihira's daughter
2 CONT Eighth Son: Imperial Prince Sadakazu (貞数親王; 875–916)
2 CONT Imperial Princess Kaneko (包子内親王; d. 889)
2 CONT Court Attendant (Koui): Fujiwara no Yoshichika's daughter
2 CONT Imperial Prince Sadahira (貞平親王; d. 914)
2 CONT Imperial Princess Shikiko (識子内親王; 874–906), 21st Saiō (Imperial Princess serving at Ise Grand Shrine) 877–880
2 CONT Court Attendant (Koui): Tachibana no Yasukage's daughter (d. 924)
2 CONT Imperial Prince Sadakata (貞固親王; 868–930)
2 CONT Court Attendant (Koui): Fujiwara no Nakamune's daughter
2 CONT third Son: Imperial Prince Sadamoto (貞元親王; 870–910)
2 CONT Court Attendant (Koui): Prince Munesada's daughter
2 CONT Sixth Son: Imperial Prince Sadasumi (貞純親王; 873–916) – father of Minamoto no Tsunemoto, founder of the Seiwa Genji, from whom the Kamakura shogunate, Ashikaga shogunate and the Tokugawa shogunate descend.
2 CONT Court Attendant (Koui): Fujiwara no Sadamune's daughter
2 CONT Imperial Prince Sadayori (貞頼親王; 876–922)
2 CONT Court Attendant (Koui): Fujiwara no Morofuji's daughter
2 CONT Imperial Prince Sadazane (貞真親王; 876–932)
2 CONT Court Attendant (Koui): Fujiwara no Morokazu's daughter
2 CONT Imperial Princess Mōshi (孟子内親王; d. 901)
2 CONT Court Attendant (Koui): Saeki no Sanefusa's daughter
2 CONT Minamoto no Nagami (源長鑒)
2 CONT Minamoto no Nagayori (源長頼; b. 875)
2 CONT Court Attendant (Koui): Ben-no-miyasundokoro (弁の御息所), Ōe no Otondo's daughter
2 CONT Court lady: Kamo no Mineo's daughter
2 CONT Minamoto no Naganori (源長猷; d. 918)
2 CONT Minamoto no Saishi/Noriko (源載子)
2 CONT Court lady: Ōno no Takatori's daughter
2 CONT Minamoto no Nagafuchi (源長淵)
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/122572206/emperor-seiwa
