1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Prince Minu (also called Prince Mino) was a descendant of Emperor Bidatsu and the son of Prince Kurikuma , who was ranked 4th . He held the rank of Junior 4th Rank, Lower Grade , and was the Minister of Justice . H
2 CONC is name is written in various ways in the Six National Histories , including Prince Minu , Prince Mino , Prince Mino , and Prince Mino , so there are problems with identifying him as a person . [ 1 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT From
2 CONT In various historical records, they are all said to be descendants of Emperor Bidatsu , but there are some differences regarding their genealogy, as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nihon Shoki [ 2 ] , Kojiki and Shinsen Shojiroku refer to him as one of three kings.
2 CONT Emperor Bidatsu - Prince Naniwa - King Kurikuma - King Minamoto
2 CONT " Sonpi Bunmyaku " describes him as the fourth king.
2 CONT Emperor Bidatsu - Prince Naniwa - King Omata - King Kurikuma - King Mino
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT When the Jinshin War broke out in June of the first year of the reign of Emperor Tenmu ( 672 ), Saeki no Otoko came to Tsukushi to levy soldiers on the orders of the Omi Court, which was on the side of Prince Otomo , but Tsukushi 
2 CONC Dazai (chief governor of Tsukushi), King Kurikuma , refused to levy soldiers, citing the need to prepare for a foreign enemy. Saeki no Otoko was also ordered to kill anyone who did not comply with the order, but he was unable to c
2 CONC arry out his mission because the king's two sons, King Mino and King Buke, were nearby, carrying swords . [ 3 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 10th year of the reign of Emperor Tenmu ( 681 ), he was ordered by the Emperor to record and revise the " Teiki " and other ancient matters together with Prince Kawashima and others . [ 4 ] In the 8th year of the reign of E
2 CONC mpress Jitō ( 694 ), he was appointed as the Dazai-to of Tsukushi ( his rank at that time was Jōkōshi).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 701 , when the Taiho Code came into force and the rank system was established, he was promoted to Junior Fifth Rank , and in the same year he was appointed Chief of the Daiheisho Office. He subsequently served as Sakyo no Daibu
2 CONC  , Settsu no Daibu, Jibukyo, and other positions , and reached the rank of Junior Fourth Rank . He passed away on May 30, 708. There is a theory that poems 3327 and 3328 in Volume 13 of the Manyoshu are long poems and tanka poems 
2 CONC about the whining of a beloved horse that has lost its king.
