2 CONT
2 CONT           o Liu Zhang (劉章), Prince Jing of Chengyang (城陽景王) (d. 177 BC), son to Liu Fei (劉肥), Prince Daohui of Qi
2 CONT
2 CONT           o Liu Xingju (劉興居), Marquess of Dongmou (d. 177 BC), son to Liu Fei (劉肥), Prince Daohui of Qi
2 CONT
2 CONT           o Liu Qi (劉啟), Crown Prince (created 179 BC d. 141 BC), later Emperor Jing of Han, son to Liu Heng (劉恆), Prince of Dai
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Was already Prince of Han (漢王) since March 206 BC, having been
2 CONT
2 CONT       enfeoffed by the rebelled leader Xiang Yu. Liu Bang was proclaimed emperor on February 28, 202 BC after defeating Xiang Yu.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ Name meaning "the youngest one". Liu Bang was the third son of his father, his oldest brother was called Bo (伯) , i.e. the "First one", and his second older brother was called Zhong (仲) , i.e. the "Middle one".
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. ^ Had his name changed into Bang, meaning "country", either when he was made Prince of Han, or when he ascended the imperial throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. ^ Ji was the courtesy name according to Sima Qian in his Records of the Grand Historian. It may be that Liu Bang, after he changed his name into Bang, kept his original name Ji as his courtesy name. However, some authors do 
2 CONC not think that "Ji" was ever used as the courtesy name of Liu Bang.
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. ^ This is the birth year reported by Huangfu Mi (皇甫謐) (215-282), the famous author of acupuncture books.
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. ^ This is the birth year reported by Chen Zan (臣瓚) around AD 270 in his comments of the Book of Han (漢書) .
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Needham, Joseph (1986). Science and Civilization in China: Volume 4, Part 2. Taipei: Caves Books, Ltd.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://cbdb.fas.harvard.edu/cbdbapi/person.php?id=16622 '''Liu Bang''' 劉邦] [16622] Han Gaozu.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://historica.fandom.com/wiki/Liu_Bang
2 CONT
2 CONT Liu Bang (247 BC-1 June 195 BC), also known as Emperor Gaozu of Han, was the Emperor of the Han dynasty from 202 BC to 195 BC, preceding Emperor Hui of Han. Originally a leader of the rebellion against the Qin dynasty, he became t
2 CONC he king of Hanzhong before defeating Western Chu to form the Han dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT His only daughter, married to Modu, the Chanyu of the great Hunnic empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Liu Bang was born in 247 BC, the son of Liu Taigong, and he was originally a soldier of the State of Chu before joining Xiang Yu's rebellion against the Qin dynasty in 207 BC. In 206, Xiang Yu divided the Qin into the Eighteen Kin
2 CONC gdoms, with Liu Bang leading the state of Han in Hanzhong. Liu Bang expanded his kingdom, and soon, he was at odds with Xiang Yu's Western Chu in the Chu-Han Contention. By 202 BC, Liu Bang had emerged victorious and Xiang Yu had 
2 CONC been killed by a defecting soldier. Taking on the regnal name of "Gaozu", Liu Bang courted popularity and consolidated their rule by denouncing the harshness of the Qin and renouncing Qin laws. Stories stress his fondness for drin
2 CONC k, blunt speech, and easy manner, and he was a man of the people. His descendant Liu Bei sought to be like him, being benevolent and later becoming King of Hanzhong.
